[{:round 1, :record {:interface "Construct a network 'print-quote' that accepts an order struct and a pricing struct. It reads order slots (:quantity, :rush?, :member?, :discount-cap) and pricing slots (tier limits/prices, rates, fees, threshold). It computes progressive production via select/min/max on quantity against tier boundaries, applies rush surcharge conditionally, applies member discount conditionally with min-cap, adds setup fee, determines delivery via select on subtotal vs threshold, computes tax, and returns a struct with :production :rush :discount :subtotal :delivery :tax :total. The network is attached to shared-rule via ttms-premise-closure. For the current question, invoke it with order-1 and pricing, then connect results to quote-1 slots and user-output-1. This is a new construction because no prior callable exists; the pricing and order compounds are data containers, not computations.", :problem-class "Print-shop quote computation: progressive-tier production pricing with conditional rush surcharge, conditional member discount (capped), fixed setup fee, conditional delivery waiver, and percentage tax. The class covers any order quantity, rush/standard service, and member/nonmember status.", :relations "Production: progressive tiers. Rush: rush-rate * production if rush. Discount: member-rate * (production+rush) if member, capped by discount-cap. Subtotal: production + rush - discount + setup-fee. Delivery: delivery-fee if subtotal < free-delivery-threshold, else 0. Tax: tax-rate * (subtotal + delivery). Total: subtotal + delivery + tax, rounded to cents.", :tests "Verify production=160, rush=0, discount=0, subtotal=185, delivery=12, tax=15.76, total=212.76 for this input. A general test with quantity>100 would exercise tier-two. A test with rush=true and member=true would exercise surcharge and discount paths.", :captured "Shared pricing rules captured in the pricing compound: tier-one-limit, tier-one-price, tier-two-count, tier-two-price, tier-three-price, rush-rate, member-rate, setup-fee, delivery-fee, free-delivery-threshold, tax-rate. These remain constant across all orders and are referenced from the pricing struct rather than passed as arguments.", :steps "1. Compute progressive production cost for 80 copies (all in tier-one since 80<=100). 2. Compute rush charge (0 since standard). 3. Compute discount (0 since nonmember). 4. Compute subtotal = production + rush - discount + setup-fee. 5. Determine delivery (12 since subtotal < 250). 6. Compute tax = tax-rate * (subtotal + delivery). 7. Compute total = subtotal + delivery + tax, round to cents. 8. Connect all values to quote-1 slots and produce text reply.", :operators "struct, slot, +, -, *, /, min, max, select, >=, <=, =, round-money, str, network, define, ttms-premise-closure", :status "ready", :outputs "A quote struct (quote-1) with slots :production, :rush, :discount, :subtotal, :delivery, :tax, :total, and a text reply (user-output-1) showing the charges and final total.", :operator "print-quote", :units "Copies (integer), dollars (decimal), rates (dimensionless fractions).", :unknowns "None for this question; all inputs are known.", :facts "quantity-1=80, rush-1=false, member-1=false, discount-cap-1=40. tier-one-limit=100, tier-one-price=2, tier-two-count=400, tier-two-price=1.60M, tier-three-price=1.30M, rush-rate=0.20M, member-rate=0.10M, setup-fee=25, delivery-fee=12, free-delivery-threshold=250, tax-rate=0.08M. No prior source code exists. pricing compound exposes shared numeric rules. order-1 compound exposes per-order inputs.", :parameters "Per-order varying inputs: quantity (integer copies), rush? (boolean), member? (boolean), discount-cap (dollar cap on member discount). These are read from the order-N struct slots.", :decision :construct}, :selected-reference nil, :selected-callable false, :selection-validation :unavailable-or-not-callable, :request-count 2, :identity {:id #uuid "40778142-2984-45db-83cc-2ca03fddcf57", :run-id #uuid "